---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CriticalityDivider from '../../components/CriticalityDivider.astro';
import InterferometerDiagram from '../../components/InterferometerDiagram.astro';
import cavityResponse from '../../assets/research/interferometry/cavity_response.png';
import mziTransfer from '../../assets/research/interferometry/mzi_transfer.png';
import fringesComplementary from '../../assets/research/interferometry/lab/fringes_complementary.png';
import armImbalance from '../../assets/research/interferometry/lab/arm_imbalance.png';
import aomDelayPositive from '../../assets/research/interferometry/lab/aom_delay_positive.png';
import aomDelayNegative from '../../assets/research/interferometry/lab/aom_delay_negative.png';
import countDrift from '../../assets/research/interferometry/lab/count_drift.png';
import lockInjectionTimeline from '../../assets/research/interferometry/lab/lock_injection_timeline.png';
import lockPerformance from '../../assets/research/interferometry/lab/lock_performance.png';
import phaseLinearity from '../../assets/research/interferometry/lab/phase_linearity.png';
import phaseLinearityDifferential from '../../assets/research/interferometry/lab/phase_linearity_differential.png';
import cavityReflectionDip from '../../assets/research/interferometry/lab/cavity_reflection_dip.png';
import fiberCouplingMicrograph from '../../assets/research/interferometry/lab/fiber_coupling_micrograph.png';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Base
  title="Phase interferometry for nanophotonic cavities"
  description="A room-temperature, all-fibre Mach–Zehnder interferometer I built and locked to read out the reflection phase of a nanophotonic cavity, resolving the over- versus under-coupled degeneracy that reflected intensity alone cannot."
>
  <article class="shell">
    <p class="back"><a href={base + '/research'}>← Research</a></p>

    <header class="proj-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">2026 · Lukin group, Harvard</p>
      <h1>Phase Interferometry for Room-Temperature Characterization of Nanophotonic Cavities</h1>
      <p class="proj-blurb">
        A cavity's reflected <em>intensity</em> cannot tell you whether it is over- or
        under-coupled — both regimes produce the same dip. The reflected <em>phase</em> can.
        I built an all-fibre Mach–Zehnder interferometer to read that phase out on the bench,
        at room temperature, phase-locked it against acoustic and thermal drift with two AOMs
        and a Red Pitaya servo, and validated the readout to sub-degree precision by injecting
        a known phase and measuring it back.
      </p>

      <dl class="proj-meta">
        <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Visiting Undergraduate Research Fellow</dd></div>
        <div>
          <dt>Topics</dt>
          <dd class="tags">
            <span class="tag">fibre interferometry</span>
            <span class="tag">cavity QED</span>
            <span class="tag">AOM phase locking</span>
            <span class="tag">Red Pitaya servo</span>
            <span class="tag">nanophotonics</span>
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The measurement problem</h2>
      <p>
        A single-sided cavity — one intentional input port with rate <em>κ</em><sub>ext</sub>,
        plus unavoidable internal loss <em>κ</em><sub>int</sub> — has a reflection coefficient
        that follows from input–output theory:
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        <em>r</em><sub>c</sub>(<em>ω</em>) = 1 −
        <span class="frac"><span class="num"><em>κ</em><sub>ext</sub></span><span class="den"><em>i</em>(<em>ω</em> − <em>ω</em><sub>0</sub>) + <em>κ</em>/2</span></span>,
        <span class="eq-where">κ = κ<sub>ext</sub> + κ<sub>int</sub></span>
      </div>

      <p>
        On resonance this collapses to a purely real number whose <em>sign</em> carries the
        physics:
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        <em>r</em><sub>c</sub>(<em>ω</em><sub>0</sub>) =
        <span class="frac"><span class="num"><em>κ</em><sub>int</sub> − <em>κ</em><sub>ext</sub></span><span class="den"><em>κ</em><sub>int</sub> + <em>κ</em><sub>ext</sub></span></span>
      </div>

      <p>
        An <strong>over-coupled</strong> cavity (<em>κ</em><sub>ext</sub> &gt; <em>κ</em><sub>int</sub>)
        gives <em>r</em><sub>c</sub> &lt; 0; an <strong>under-coupled</strong> one gives
        <em>r</em><sub>c</sub> &gt; 0. Because a detector measures
        |<em>r</em><sub>c</sub>|<sup>2</sup>, that sign is invisible: a cavity with
        <em>κ</em><sub>ext</sub>/<em>κ</em> = 0.8 and one with 0.2 produce identical reflection
        dips. Phase does not hide it — sweeping across resonance, an over-coupled cavity winds
        <em>φ</em><sub>c</sub> by a full 2π, while an under-coupled cavity makes a small
        excursion and returns to where it started. Resolving that winding, at room temperature,
        before a device is committed to a cryostat, is what this interferometer is for.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={cavityResponse} alt="Two-panel plot: reflected intensity versus detuning, where over- and under-coupled cavities overlap exactly; and reflection phase versus detuning, where the over-coupled case winds by 2 pi while the under-coupled case barely moves." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Computed for <em>κ</em><sub>ext</sub>/<em>κ</em> = 0.8 (over-coupled), 0.5
          (critical), 0.2 (under-coupled). <strong>Top:</strong> the over- and under-coupled
          intensity traces coincide exactly. <strong>Bottom:</strong> the same two cases are
          immediately distinguishable in phase — the degeneracy this interferometer exists to
          break.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <CriticalityDivider label="the interferometer" />

      <h2>The setup</h2>
      <p>
        The instrument is an all-fibre Mach–Zehnder built on the bench, no cryostat involved.
        The cavity sits in the signal arm behind a circulator; the reference arm carries the
        local oscillator through a variable delay stage and an adjustable attenuator that
        matches the per-arm power. The arms recombine on a 50:50 coupler and are read out on
        two photodiodes — PDs, not APDs, since the fringes here are large classical voltages
        and what matters is linear, high-bandwidth detection, not photon counting. The PD
        difference drives a servo on a Red Pitaya that closes the loop onto two in-fibre AOMs,
        one per arm.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <InterferometerDiagram />
        <figcaption>
          Setup, redrawn from my own sketch: signal arm (AOM → tap → circulator → cavity) and
          reference arm (AOM → delay stage → attenuator), recombined onto photodiodes PD1/PD2
          whose difference drives the Red Pitaya servo phase-locking the two AOMs.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Reading phase out</h2>
      <p>
        Phase is not directly observable; it has to be converted into intensity by
        interference against the reference. Recombining the two arms gives two complementary
        outputs,
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        <em>I</em><sub>±</sub> ∝ |<em>E</em><sub>S</sub>|<sup>2</sup> + |<em>E</em><sub>L</sub>|<sup>2</sup>
        ± 2|<em>E</em><sub>S</sub>||<em>E</em><sub>L</sub>| cosΔ<em>φ</em>
      </div>

      <p>
        so the <em>difference</em> of the two photodiodes isolates the interference term and
        rejects common-mode intensity noise. The measured phase is a sum of three
        contributions,
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        Δ<em>φ</em>(<em>ω</em>) = <em>φ</em><sub>c</sub>(<em>ω</em>) + <em>ω</em>Δ<em>τ</em> + <em>δφ</em><sub>AOM</sub>
      </div>

      <p>
        of which only the first is wanted; the second is the propagation phase of any arm
        imbalance and the third is a control knob — both have to be characterised and removed
        before <em>φ</em><sub>c</sub> means anything.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={mziTransfer} alt="Two-panel plot: complementary MZI output intensities I+ and I- versus differential phase, showing offset cosine fringes; and fringe visibility versus arm delay mismatch in units of coherence time, showing a Gaussian roll-off." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Computed transfer function. <strong>Left:</strong> <em>I</em><sub>+</sub> and
          <em>I</em><sub>−</sub> versus differential phase. <strong>Right:</strong> visibility
          rolling off as the arm delay mismatch approaches the laser coherence time.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The real fringes look exactly like this: sweeping the optical phase at six injected
        offsets from 0° to 150° traces out the same cosine, successively shifted — raw
        confirmation that the transfer function above is what the hardware actually does.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={fringesComplementary} alt="Top: overlapping interference fringes (PD1 minus PD2) versus swept optical phase for six injected phase offsets from 0 to 150 degrees. Bottom: the two complementary photodiode voltages versus applied phase, out of phase with each other." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Measured fringes. <strong>Top:</strong> PD-difference signal versus swept phase for
          injected offsets 0°–150°. <strong>Bottom:</strong> the two individual photodiode
          traces beneath one such sweep, out of phase — the raw complementary outputs the lock
          rides on.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Path matching and the arm imbalance</h2>
      <p>
        An arm-length mismatch Δ<em>L</em> contributes a delay
        Δ<em>τ</em> = <em>n</em>Δ<em>L</em>/<em>c</em>, which appears as a linear ramp in
        <em>ω</em>Δ<em>τ</em> — fringes periodic in laser frequency with free spectral range
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        FSR = <span class="frac"><span class="num"><em>c</em></span><span class="den"><em>n</em>Δ<em>L</em></span></span>
        ⟹ Δ<em>L</em> = <span class="frac"><span class="num"><em>c</em></span><span class="den"><em>n</em>·FSR</span></span>
      </div>

      <p>
        which makes the FSR a ruler for the imbalance. I added roughly 10 m of extra fibre to
        one arm to bring the two paths into rough length match; fibre-length bookkeeping put
        the residual imbalance at Δ<em>L</em> ≈ −1.915 m, to be closed out with the delay
        stage. I checked that number independently by timing the per-arm pulse arrival
        directly — launching a short pulse down each arm and fitting a Gaussian/erf-box
        lineshape to each arrival — which gave <em>dt</em> = −9.5495 ns →
        Δ<em>L</em> = −1.9515 m, agreeing with the fibre bookkeeping to within about 3.6 cm.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={armImbalance} alt="Per-arm pulse arrival time plot: two Gaussian peaks slightly offset in time, with erf-box fits, labeled dt = -9.5495 ns, dL = -1.9515 m." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Per-arm pulse arrival, normalised counts versus time since sync. Gaussian/erf-box
          fits to arm 1 and arm 2 give <em>dt</em> = −9.5495 ns →
          Δ<em>L</em> = −1.9515 m, matching the fibre-length target of −1.915 m.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The same pulse-arrival measurement done straight off the two AOMs, bypassing the rest
        of the interferometer, told a different story: each AOM has an intrinsic acoustic
        delay, the transit time for the RF drive to propagate across the acousto-optic
        interaction region before it modulates the light. The tell was that swapping which
        channel of the signal generator (SDG) drove which AOM flipped the sign of the measured
        delay — from <em>dt</em> = +40.9949 ns → Δ<em>L</em> = +8.3777 m to
        <em>dt</em> = −41.0286 ns → Δ<em>L</em> = −8.3846 m — with the fibre paths themselves
        untouched. A genuine path-length asymmetry cannot care which electrical channel drives
        which AOM; a delay that tracks the channel assignment instead has to live inside the
        AOM/RF chain, and its further dependence on drive voltage confirms an acousto-electronic
        origin. Left uncorrected it enters Δ<em>φ</em>(<em>ω</em>) indistinguishably from a
        genuine <em>ω</em>Δ<em>τ</em> arm mismatch, biasing any coupling ratio extracted from a
        real cavity.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={aomDelayPositive} alt="Per-arm pulse arrival plot with AOMs connected directly to detectors: dt = +40.9949 ns, dL = +8.3777 m." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          AOMs driven directly into the detectors, signal generator on its original channel
          assignment: <em>dt</em> = +40.9949 ns → Δ<em>L</em> = +8.3777 m.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={aomDelayNegative} alt="Same measurement with the signal generator channels swapped between the two AOMs: dt = -41.0286 ns, dL = -8.3846 m, sign flipped relative to the previous figure." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          SDG channels swapped between the two AOMs: <em>dt</em> = −41.0286 ns →
          Δ<em>L</em> = −8.3846 m. The sign flip, fibre untouched, signs the delay as internal
          to the AOMs.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <CriticalityDivider label="locking" />

      <h2>Locking the fringe</h2>
      <p>
        A fibre interferometer drifts — thermal expansion and acoustic pickup move
        Δ<em>φ</em> continuously, and the detected count rate wanders with it, so an unlocked
        instrument measures nothing. The actuators are the two AOMs: an AOM driven at
        <em>V</em><sub>RF</sub>(<em>t</em>) = <em>V</em><sub>0</sub>cos(Ω<sub>RF</sub><em>t</em> + <em>δφ</em><sub>AOM</sub>)
        transfers the instantaneous RF phase onto the first-order diffracted beam,
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        <em>E</em><sub>1st</sub>(<em>t</em>) ∝ <em>E</em><sub>in</sub>(<em>t</em>)
        <em>e</em><sup><em>i</em>(Ω<sub>RF</sub><em>t</em> + <em>δφ</em><sub>AOM</sub>)</sup>
      </div>

      <p>
        Driving the two arms on opposite diffraction orders turns one common RF phase into a
        differential optical phase, Δ<em>φ</em><sub>AOM</sub> = 2<em>δφ</em><sub>AOM</sub>, so
        a single number written to the Red Pitaya steers the fringe directly.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={countDrift} alt="Detector count rate versus time on two channels, wandering continuously over tens of seconds." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Detector count rate versus time on two channels, unlocked — the drift the servo has
          to fight.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Locking and probing are interleaved rather than simultaneous. One capture cycle
        freezes the PID, holds the servo on its plateau, then the SDG writes a new phase step
        — in one annotated cycle the write itself took 13.3 ms between the SCPI command being
        issued and returning — after which the optical phase visibly jumps and scanning
        resumes with the injected phase in effect. That interleaving is what lets a
        fixed-frequency lock coexist with a phase deliberately stepped for calibration.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={lockInjectionTimeline} alt="Annotated timeline of one capture cycle: PIDs frozen, servo plateau locked, SDG issuing and completing a phase write over 13.3 milliseconds, then the optical phase visibly jumping, then scanning resuming." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          One capture cycle, annotated: locked plateau, SDG phase write (13.3 ms), the phase
          visibly jumping, then scanning continuing with the new phase in effect.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The lock itself came together in two steps. On 13 July 2026, turning off the phase
        modulation on channel 1 and recalibrating produced the first successful lock. On 17
        July 2026, I locked the full arm — delay stage and fibre spool included — using the
        photodiodes, taking the loop from an oscillating fringe to a flat, held plateau.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={lockPerformance} alt="Two-panel plot: upper panel shows two photodiode voltages oscillating widely then snapping to a flat locked plateau; lower panel shows the actuator voltage jumping then settling into small correction excursions." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Lock performance, 17 July 2026: error signal (upper, two photodiode channels)
          swinging widely unlocked, then snapping onto a flat plateau; actuator voltage
          (lower) making an initial correction and settling into small excursions.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Validating the phase readout</h2>
      <p>
        To know whether the locked instrument reads phase correctly, I injected a known
        optical phase — 0°, 30°, 60°, 90°, 120°, 150° — through the AOM RF phase and measured
        it back through the lock. The first pass, reading the phase directly against a
        recorded start voltage, tracked the ideal <em>y</em> = <em>x</em> line on average but
        with visible scatter between repeated cycles at a given injected phase.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={phaseLinearity} alt="Scatter plot of injected phase versus probed phase in degrees, with per-cycle measurements scattered around the ideal y = x line and per-point means tracking it." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Injected versus probed phase, first pass: per-cycle measurements (points) and
          per-point means (diamonds) against the ideal <em>y</em> = <em>x</em> line, at
          injected phases of 0°–150°.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Switching to a differential, self-referenced analysis — comparing each phase step
        against its own cycle's starting point rather than an absolute recorded voltage —
        removed the slow drift that had been aliasing into the first method as scatter.
        Per-point errors across the injected range came out at roughly 0.05°–0.64°, with
        per-point standard deviations of about 0.36°–0.79° over <em>n</em> = 7 repeated
        cycles at most injected phases.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={phaseLinearityDifferential} alt="Scatter plot of injected phase versus measured phase using the differential, self-referenced method, with error and standard deviation annotated at each injected phase, points collapsed tightly onto the y = x line." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Differential self-referenced method: errors of 0.05°–0.64°, standard deviations of
          0.36°–0.79° over <em>n</em> = 7 cycles per point — a marked improvement over the
          first-pass readout above.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Toward the cavity</h2>
      <p>
        With the lock characterised and the readout validated, the remaining piece is coupling
        light into the actual nanophotonic device — fibre-to-chip alignment under a microscope,
        watched live while the fibre tip is walked onto the device edge.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={fiberCouplingMicrograph} alt="Micrograph from an alignment camera showing an optical fibre tip positioned near the edge of a nanophotonic device." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Fibre tip aligned to the device edge under the coupling microscope.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        A first reflection scan across the device shows the signature this apparatus exists to
        interpret: a clear resonance dip in the 723–727 nm range — the intensity-only view
        that leaves over- versus under-coupling undetermined until the interferometer is
        brought to bear on it.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={cavityReflectionDip} alt="Reflection scan across roughly 721 to 727.5 nanometres showing a clear dip in counts around 723 to 727 nanometres." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Reflection scan, counts versus wavelength: a resonance dip in the 723–727 nm range.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Conclusions</h2>
      <p>
        This project produced a working, room-temperature, all-fibre Mach–Zehnder
        interferometer, locked against drift with a two-AOM, Red Pitaya servo, with a phase
        readout validated to sub-degree accuracy — errors of 0.05°–0.64°, standard deviations
        of 0.36°–0.79°, over repeated calibration cycles. Getting there meant treating the arm
        imbalance and the AOM chain as separate problems: the physical path mismatch was
        pinned down twice, by fibre bookkeeping and by direct pulse-arrival timing, to
        Δ<em>L</em> ≈ −1.9 m, while the AOMs' own acoustic delay — exposed by the sign flip
        under channel swapping — had to be identified and kept from masquerading as part of
        that mismatch. The lock moved from a first success on 13 July to a full-arm lock, delay
        stage and fibre spool included, on 17 July.
      </p>
      <p>
        What limits the instrument now is largely statistics and integration: several
        calibration points still rest on a single cycle rather than the <em>n</em> = 7 used
        elsewhere, and the AOM delay's dependence on drive voltage means a change in operating
        point calls for fresh calibration rather than a fixed correction. The cavity itself has
        so far only been characterised in intensity, giving the reflection dip but not the
        coupling ratio. The natural next step is to route that cavity into the signal arm and
        sweep it under lock, using the interleaved lock/probe sequence demonstrated here to
        extract <em>φ</em><sub>c</sub>(<em>ω</em>) directly and read the winding number that
        intensity alone cannot provide.
      </p>
    </div>

    <footer class="proj-foot">
      <a href={base + '/research'}>← Back to research</a>
    </footer>
  </article>
</Base>

